[?25h[m[H[J  I named mine home, and lost it underneath the branches.[3;1H  All afternoon the current worked its patient way through roots.
  It worried at the buried stones until their edges softened.
  Our mother called; her voice came thinly through the leaves.
  We climbed the bank with river mud beneath our fingernails.
  The kitchen windows shone above us, bright with ordinary welcome.[9;1H  Now other waters move beneath the bridges of my days.
  I cross them carrying groceries, letters, things I cannot answer.
  Below, a branch revolves beside the shadow of a pillar.
  For several breaths it seems unable to escape that circle.
  Then something shifts; it turns and travels onward without ceremony.[15;1H  Far downstream, the river spreads through fields of bending reeds.
  The water tastes of distances no single mouth could name.
  Here mountain snow encounters rain that fell on city pavements.
  Here minnows shelter underneath a slowly turning raft of grass.
  And everything moves seaward, though the tide comes pushing inland.[21;1H  Tonight I leave my window open, listening for distant water.
  A truck goes by; the gutters mutter softly after rainfall.
  Somewhere a river shoulders through the dark beyond these houses.
  I picture neither journey's end nor any promise of arrival.
  Only the wet stones shining when the morning finds them.
